atomic exchange. Before invocation, it checks exact app/container locations,
device/inode identities, private staging ownership, and a fixed silent helper.
The helper rechecks directory identities through held no-follow parent
file descriptors, then performs the exchange and syncs both parent directories.
No rename-away/delete sequence or filesystem downgrade fallback is used.

Apple's [swap-renaming capability][swap-support] and
[Darwin syscall header][swap-header], checked on 2026-10-08, establish the
`RENAME_SWAP` primitive. The shared C helper uses `renameat2` with
`RENAME_EXCHANGE` only for Linux development checks; the product adapter
continues to refuse Linux as an update target. Native ARM64 compilation and
Darwin filesystem/permission assurance stay in task blooket-15.

Portable tests compile the actual helper with warnings as errors and exercise
real Linux exchanges, inverse rollback, refusal of blind repeat, lost
acknowledgements, changed identities/permissions, symbolic paths, host stubs,
and cancellation while an owned writer is still live. Cancellation waits for
writer completion and reports the observed orientation; it cannot authorize a
blind retry or deleting either retained app. An unknown outcome stays unknown.

The helper is included in both developer and Mac runtime assembly, and package
verification exercises the delivered mechanism on disposable app directories.
This is an installation primitive, not an enabled updater. Application journals,
writer quiescence, independent helper lifecycle, health-confirmed restart,
rollback/recovery, production publisher provisioning, and UI integration remain
required before functional completion.

### Publication exclusion foundation

The canonical publication command now holds a shared cross-process boundary
before resolving publication paths, snapshot creation, capability reads, or
remote work. Update composition can acquire that same exclusive ownership and
retain it through service quiescence, exchange, restart, or recovery. This
serializes publication commands while preserving their per-draft journal locks.

Before granting an update boundary, a bounded directory observer refuses any
remaining attempt file, including malformed or confirmed journals. It does not
read lesson content, alter the journal, or replay a write. Symbolic storage,
unknown directories/files, stale inner locks, oversized catalogs, and I/O
failures cannot authorize installation; failed admission releases its ownership.

Portable tests exercise active publication refusal, blocked new commands,
retained uncertain attempts, explicit reconciliation, lock lifetime, and unsafe
storage. Node's [directory iteration contract][directory-iteration], checked on
2026-10-08, establishes bounded streaming and iterator-owned directory closure.
This boundary is a composition prerequisite, not an enabled updater; other
persistent writers, install journals, independent helper lifecycle, production
publisher trust, safe restart/rollback, and UI integration remain pending.

### Durable installation recovery records

A schema-one installation journal records the signed envelope, original and
candidate directory identities, versions, sibling locations, and explicit
intent/observation/health phases. Its exact decoder rejects foreign locations,
changed installation IDs, contradictory device/inode facts, unsupported phases,
and non-newer candidates. Transition validation cannot skip intent or observed
ownership; decoding a signature-shaped envelope never grants publisher trust.

The private journal adapter bounds UTF-8/JSON reads to 16 KiB and retains a
cross-process installation lock. Exclusive creation preserves existing work;
compare-and-replace refuses stale or changed immutable facts and uses durable
atomic replacement. Symbolic, hard-linked, publicly readable, oversized,
malformed, or replaced storage fails closed without clearing recovery evidence.

Close drains admitted I/O before releasing ownership and refuses further work.
A failed close can retry ownership release while the store remains closed;
nonblocking no-follow reads reject special files without waiting for a writer.

Recovery assessment requires independent proof that the native writer has
stopped and fresh observation of both retained directories. Lost exchange
acknowledgements require a new health check, never a blind second exchange;
even a recorded healthy phase requires fresh health. Unknown ownership cannot
authorize removal or replacement, and rollback intent remains distinct from
observed restoration and verified old-version health.

Portable tests persist intent around a real Linux native exchange, reopen the
journal before recording completion, perform the guarded inverse rollback,
and verify both application trees remain. Additional cases cover a separate
process, stale transitions, corrupted bytes, storage permissions, and close
ordering. Node's [filesystem contract][directory-iteration], checked on
2026-10-08, establishes bigint identity and nanosecond timestamp reads; these
checks do not claim native Darwin acceptance or an enabled product updater.

Independent installer lifecycle, other persistent-writer fencing, candidate
preparation/Apple reassessment, production publisher trust, actual service
restart/health, and local UI/opt-in composition remain functional work.

### Managed service writer drain

A regression on 2026-10-08 reproduced managed shutdown releasing its service
lock while an admitted settings request was still inside an asynchronous secret
write. The loopback service now closes admission, interrupts pending browser
and update reads, closes transport connections, and waits for every admitted
handler before managed lifecycle removes runtime discovery or releases
ownership.
Socket closure is never treated as completion of a filesystem/native writer.

Portable real-HTTP tests hold a settings writer across shutdown, verify a second
service cannot acquire ownership, complete and preserve the settings write,
and then restart successfully. Another case verifies resource-close failure
still drains an ignoring request and reports failure only afterward. Node's
[HTTP shutdown contract][http-shutdown], checked on 2026-10-08, supports closing
the server before all connections; application handler ownership is tracked
separately and close attempts are coalesced.

This is a managed local-service prerequisite. Independent CLI writers, the
online gateway's admitted child operations, staged installer lifetime, publisher
trust, and end-to-end updater composition still need their own admission and
recovery guarantees before installation can be enabled.

### Canonical child completion

Regression coverage on 2026-10-08 reproduced canonical CLI promises settling
on timeout, oversized output, or spawn errors before child process closure.
The CLI boundary now retains ownership until `close`, preserves the first
failure, discards further output, and handles output-stream errors. A late
successful envelope cannot override an earlier failure or authorize a retry.

Synthetic lifecycle tests distinguish signal delivery, process exit, and final
closure; existing real CLI/MCP publication coverage still traverses the actual
subprocess. Node's [child-process lifecycle contract][child-completion], checked
on 2026-10-08, distinguishes those events. This proves closure of the canonical
child only; independent native descendants require their own completion proof.

### Online admitted-command drain

On 2026-10-08 the authenticated gateway gained a local quiescence operation:
close admission, revoke grants, close connections, and await all admitted
handlers before completing. Repeated calls share completion. A disconnected
client cannot abandon its canonical child command.

Online lifecycle starts that drain before waiting for tunnel shutdown and
reports tunnel failure only after the gateway drain finishes. Real OAuth/HTTP
coverage holds a synthetic canonical child through disconnect and process exit,
then proves shutdown waits for final closure; existing calls still execute the
real CLI. Composition tests cover a stalled/failing tunnel and prevent reload
after final stop.

Independent CLI writers, native descendants, staged installer lifecycle, and
end-to-end update composition remain pending. This portable prerequisite does
not enable installation or assert publisher trust, actual provider publication,
or native macOS acceptance.

### Exact queued installation intent (2026-10-09)

Regressions reproduced caller mutation before queued journal I/O: creation
could persist changed directory identities and signed-envelope facts,
replacement could reinterpret its selected phase, and invalid inputs could
become admitted after the call. The journal adapter now decodes and copies both
creation and compare-and-replace inputs synchronously before queue admission or
filesystem awaits. Invalid initial phases remain rejected, and queued writes
retain exactly the original decoded intent.

The focused suite also retains the real Linux exchange/inverse rollback test,
private storage, compare-and-replace, cross-process exclusion, and close-drain
coverage. These are portable journal guarantees, not publisher authentication,
service restart, native Mac acceptance, or an enabled product installer. The
remaining application composition and production trust requirements are
unchanged.

### Journaled installation application controller (2026-10-09)

`install.ts` under the application-update application role now coordinates
prepared authority, durable intent, fresh directory orientation, publisher
reassessment, quiescence, health, and guarded inverse recovery. Independently
prepared authority is copied before asynchronous admission; neither
journal paths nor persisted healthy phases grant trust or current health.

One forward exchange follows durable intent and a second candidate assessment
after quiescence. A lost acknowledgement is resolved through fresh orientation,
not blind replay. Failed/canceled candidate startup durably records rollback
intent, reassesses the retained old app, quiesces writers, and observes the
inverse before checking old health. Each health challenge uses a fresh nonce
and exact expected version.

Cancellation during health cannot commit a late
candidate success. Unknown identities, incomplete native writers, failed
directory sync, lost ownership, unsafe journal state, and failed health retain
both app trees and their recovery record.

Thirty-one targeted regressions pass, including real private journal storage,
held publication/journal locks, actual compiled Linux exchanges and inverse
rollback, and ephemeral Ed25519 manifest verification. Lifecycle and Apple
assessment are explicitly synthetic: these tests do not prove actual app
restart, Apple acceptance, production keys, or the shipping updater.

Synthetic
user-data bytes remain unchanged through success and failure. Interrupted intent
and healthy phases are reassessed; concurrent calls coalesce without releasing
ownership before an admitted writer closes. The [Node child-process lifecycle
contract][child-process-current] was checked on 2026-10-09.

No product entrypoint/default ports were added. An independent installer must
compose actual preparation, all persistent-writer fences, publisher assessment,
bounded descendant supervision, silent restart, and authenticated runtime health
before enabling this controller. Signed production artifacts and opt-in EN/ES
controls also remain functional requirements. Native Mac/Safari execution alone
remains in blooket-15 and does not block this portable implementation.

## Blockers

On 2026-10-13, direct local HTTP data operations began sharing canonical
user-data writer leases, retained until their handlers finish. A held update
boundary refuses settings/media operations and lazy reads while keeping pure
service/update-status observations available. Canonical publication retains
its existing ownership order, avoiding a data-before-publication inversion.

Real local service tests demonstrate that a held settings writer excludes
installation before shutdown begins, and remains protected through drainage.
A separate HTTP test proves settings and secret stores remain untouched while
installation admission is held. Ordinary managed startup and shutdown now
retain a lifecycle data lease across online operations and discovery writes.

The installer must drain the owned old service before acquiring exclusive data
admission. Private installer-owned startup can now authenticate the actual
parent's held marker and register a candidate's lifetime lease without opening
ordinary writer admission. Independent installer lifetime, publisher trust,
and product restart/recovery still need composition; these tests do not enable
automatic installation.

The standalone launcher's manual diagnostics also acquire data admission before
first-use settings or diagnostic persistence. A real launcher subprocess test
under held installation admission preserves an existing diagnostic cache and
refuses both settings initialization and runtime discovery.

Production publisher keys/manifest publication, product staging integration,
independent installer composition with all writer fences and actual runtime
health, and opt-in product controls remain functional work. Existing identity,
atomic exchange, journal, and orchestration primitives still need production
composition. Platform-only signing/notarization and native acceptance belong to
task blooket-15.

